    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d297">

                <pb facs="0789" n="753" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v07p1/pg753"/>

                <head>297. Letter From President Eisenhower to Prime Minister Diefenbaker<anchor
                        n="0" xml:id="d297-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1959-03-10">March 10, 1959</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear John</hi>: I am today signing a Proclamation
                    adjusting and regulating imports of crude oil and its principal products into
                    the United States.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d297-f2"/> The basis for this new
                    program is a certification by the Office of Civil and Defense Mobilization that
                    such action is required in the interests of our national defense.</p>
                <p>I wish to assure you that in the formulation of this program, every effort was
                    made to insure that its practical effect upon imports from <pb facs="0790"
                        n="754" xml:id="pg754"/> Canada would be minimized, and it is my
                    understanding that there will be no appreciable change in the level of such
                    imports as a result of the new program.</p>
                <p>In view of the joint interest in hemispheric defense that we share with Canada
                    and with the other American Republics, it is my sincere hope that in the near
                    future the informal conversations that have already begun will result in an
                    agreed hemispheric approach to this problem.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d297-f3"/></p>
                <p>With warm regard,</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Dwight D. Eisenhower</hi><anchor n="3"
                            xml:id="d297-f4"/></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d297-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d297-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Eisenhower Library, White House Office, Project Clean Up. No
                    classification marking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d297-f2" n="1" xml:id="d297-f2-content">
                <p>For text of Proclamation No. 3279 governing oil imports and President
                    Eisenhower’s statement at the time of its signing, see <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >American Foreign Policy: Current Documents, 1959</hi>, pp. 1455–1462.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d297-f3" n="2" xml:id="d297-f3-content">
                <p>Discussions with representatives of the Canadian and Venezuelan Embassies began
                    in January but were suspended pending installation of the new Venezuelan
                    Government in February. A report on these conversations was sent to the
                    President by Herter on March 30. (Eisenhower Library, Herter Papers)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d297-f4" n="3" xml:id="d297-f4-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that bears this typed signature.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
